---
source: crates/standalone/fixture/src/lib.rs
---
Send the folder to its site and start a deploy.

Usage: ink push [OPTIONS]

Examples
  # Send the folder and wait for the deploy
  ink push

  # Queue the build and print its id
  ink push --no-wait

Options
      --no-wait          Return when the build is queued,
                         without waiting for it
      --wait <SECONDS>   Seconds to wait for the build
                         before stopping; it keeps going on
                         the server [default: 300]
      --poll <MS>        Milliseconds between two looks at
                         the build [default: 1000]
      --batch <BYTES>    Most bytes of files in one request;
                         the server's own limit applies when
                         it is smaller
      --batch-files <N>  Most files in one request; the
                         server's own limit applies when it
                         is smaller [default: 1000]
      --rounds <N>       How many times the tree is sent and
                         the files it lacks answered, before
                         giving up [default: 3]

Account
      --api <URL>
        The API's address, `http://host[:port]`;
        `account.api` in the config file [env: INK_API]
      --site <ID>
        The site's id; `account.site` in the config file
        [env: INK_SITE]
      --org <ID>
        Act as this organization, by its id: the site is one
        of its sites, and the key's role there is what is
        checked. See `ink org list` [env: INK_ORG]
      --timeout <SECONDS>
        Seconds one call may take [default: 30]
        [env: INK_TIMEOUT]
      --retries <N>
        How many more times a call that is safe to repeat is
        tried when the network fails [default: 2]
        [env: INK_RETRIES]
      --retry-wait <MS>
        Milliseconds before the first retry; each wait is
        twice the one before [default: 500]
        [env: INK_RETRY_WAIT]

Notes
  Sends only the files the site does not hold, so an
  interrupted push is resumed by running it again. Waits for
  the build and prints the deploy id; --no-wait prints the
  build id. A key that may deploy and not read (a CI deploy
  key made with no `--right`) can only push with --no-wait.
  A deploy that is recorded and not live exits 5.

Exit codes
  0  Done: the deploy is live
  1  The build was refused: the site does not build
  2  Wrong usage: the folder is linked to no site, or the
     folder cannot be read
  3  Not signed in, or the signature was refused
  4  The API could not be reached, the build failed or the
     wait ran out; safe to run again
  5  The deploy is recorded and not live, because publishing
     is paused after a rollback

Environment
  INK_API       The address of the API, as --api
  INK_SITE      The site's id, as --site
  INK_KEY       The private key to sign requests with, for a
                CI job; never a flag, so it stays out of
                process lists. `ink login` keeps one in the
                config folder instead
  INK_KEY_FILE  A file that holds that key, in place of
                INK_KEY

See also  ink status, ink watch, ink deploy
Docs      https://github.com/uses-ink/uses.ink/blob/main/docs/cli.md
Global    -C, --json, -q, -v, --color, --no-progress, --log,
          --log-file (see `ink --help`)
